  <RESPONSE>
    Nothing purpose-built exists for Jellyfin → KDE screensaver. Here's the landscape and your real options:
    
    ---
    
    ## What exists (nothing quite right)
    
    - **KDE's built-in Slideshow wallpaper plugin** – works great, but only reads from local folders, not URLs
    - **Jellyfin web UI screensaver** – only activates inside the browser tab, not system-wide
    - No known KDE plasma plugin integrates with Jellyfin directly
    
    ---
    
    ## Three ways to build it
    
    ### Option 1 — Sync script + native KDE slideshow (simplest, most reliable)
    A small script/systemd timer calls the Jellyfin REST API (`/Users/{id}/Items` filtered to your photo library, then `/Items/{id}/Images/Primary`), downloads images to `~/.local/share/jellyfin-wallpapers/`, and prunes old ones. KDE's built-in Slideshow plugin watches that folder.
    
    - **Pros**: No custom code running at lock time, dead simple, uses proven KDE tooling
    - **Cons**: Small delay before new images appear, disk usage, not truly "live"
    
    ### Option 2 — Custom KDE Plasma wallpaper plugin (most integrated)
    A QML-based wallpaper plugin (Plasma 6 supports this natively) that fetches Jellyfin images directly via `XMLHttpRequest` and cycles them. Can be applied to both the desktop and the lock screen wallpaper slot.
    
    - **Pros**: Fully native, works on lock screen, no local disk sync needed, configurable via System Settings
    - **Cons**: Requires writing QML + a `metadata.json` package; ~200-300 lines of code
    
    ### Option 3 — xss-lock + standalone app
    A small Python/Qt or Electron app that triggers as the screensaver via `xss-lock` (X11) or a custom kscreenlocker theme.
    
    - **Pros**: Most flexible, easy to write
    - **Cons**: Wayland support is awkward, less "native" feel, more moving parts
    
    ---
    
    ## My recommendation
    
    **Option 1** if you want it done fast and reliably. It's 30-50 lines of shell/Python and zero QML.
    
    **Option 2** if you want it to feel like a real KDE feature (shows on lock screen, configurable in System Settings, works cleanly on Wayland). More upfront work but the right long-term answer.
    
    The Jellyfin API is well-documented at `http://media.halfab.net/api-docs/swagger` — you'll need an API key from the dashboard and your user ID or a public token. Do you want to proceed with one of these, and if so which?
    
    Sources:
    - [The Jellyfin API - A Broad Overview](https://jmshrv.com/posts/jellyfin-api/)
    - [Plasma themes and plugins | KDE Developer](https://develop.kde.org/docs/plasma/)
    - [Templates to create your own Plasma Wallpaper plugin](https://frinring.wordpress.com/2018/04/04/templates-to-create-your-own-plasma-wallpaper-plugin/)
    - [plasma-smart-video-wallpaper-reborn (Plasma 6 example)](https://github.com/luisbocanegra/plasma-smart-video-wallpaper-reborn)
  </RESPONSE>

